    name: "Crew Cut",
    prompt: "# Crew Cut\r\n\r\nCreate a true Crew Cut defined by a short tapered side/back structure supporting a longer graduated top, with the front-top section slightly longer than the rear top so the classic forward-directed profile remains visible.\r\n\r\n## TOP GRADUATION\r\n- Keep the top clearly longer than the sides and back.\r\n- Graduate the top from slightly longer at the front hairline toward shorter at the rear crown rather than making the entire top one uniform length.\r\n- Keep the top controlled and close enough to the skull to read as a haircut, not a quiff or pompadour.\r\n- Let the front-top hair settle naturally forward or slightly upward according to the source hair's behaviour.\r\n\r\n## SIDES AND BACK\r\n- Taper the sides and nape progressively shorter while retaining believable hair coverage.\r\n- Keep the taper clean and compact but do not create a skin fade, hard disconnect, or undercut.\r\n- Preserve natural sideburn and nape transitions and realistic density around the ears.\r\n\r\n## SURFACE AND RECOGNITION\r\nUse restrained texture, realistic short-hair fibre separation, natural root density, and subtle length variation. The defining hierarchy is longer graduated top + progressively shorter sides/back + compact tapered silhouette.\r\n\r\n## NEGATIVE CONSTRAINTS\r\nNo buzz cut, fade, skin fade, undercut, disconnected crop, Caesar crop, pompadour, quiff, mohawk, bowl cut, long top, exaggerated spikes, or artificial clipper-painted surface. Do not alter the face, jaw, chin, neck, ears, skin, lighting, background, framing, perspective, or source hair colour.",
